  "slug": "sleep-maintains-82dd",
  "summary": "Recent preprints link neuronal firing, developmental wiring and circuit engagement to sleep, fear extinction and motor output.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is how defined neuronal populations and their connections establish circuit function. In the thalamus, conditional knockout of Shox2 in VPM neurons reduces tonic and burst firing of thalamocortical neurons, impairing whisker-based behavior and sleep spindles in mice. In fear learning, juvenile mice do not engage mPFC-amygdala circuitry during extinction, and optogenetic potentiation of this pathway accelerates adult-type extinction. A developmental timeline of lateral olfactory tract innervation of piriform cortex is mapped from embryonic to postnatal stages using calretinin and tdTomato labeling. Corticofugal output neurons arising from direct versus indirect neurogenesis differ transcriptomically and are compared functionally in motor cortex during a reach-grasp-drink task. The most mechanistically specific evidence comes from the thalamic and prefrontal items, where manipulation of firing or pathway activity is tied to behavior; the olfactory and corticofugal items are primarily descriptive or lineage-based.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
